<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Berkeley, 1st Baron Berkeley of Stratton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1602–1678</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Berkeley, 1st Baron Berkeley of Stratton, was an English Royalist commander and courtier. After serving Charles I in the Civil War and the exiled royal court, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-carteret">Sir George Carteret</a> received the proprietorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> from the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> in 1664. In 1674 he sold his half share to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> John Fenwick and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-byllynge">Edward Byllynge</a>, which led to the colony's division into East and West Jersey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-berkeley-1st-baron-berkeley-of-stratton.jpg" alt="John Berkeley, 1st Baron Berkeley of Stratton" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Perfect Harding · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1stLordBerkeley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
